                                        <div class="ielts-reading-question-section-content">
                                            <div class="ielts-reading-section-content-wrapper"><p><em>Complete the summary using the list of phrases, <strong>A-J</strong>, below.</em></p>
<p><em>Write the correct letter, <strong>A-J</strong>, in boxes on your answer sheet.</em></p>
<p class="ielts-reading-passage-subhead"><strong><strong>The bedrock myth</strong></strong></p>
<p>In chapter seven, Barr indicates how the lack of bedrock close to the surface does not explain why skyscrapers are absent from <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">36</strong><select data-qnum="36" name="question_36" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 36 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. development plans">A. development plans</option><option value="B. deep excavations">B. deep excavations</option><option value="C. great distance">C. great distance</option><option value="D. excessive expense">D. excessive expense</option><option value="E. impossible tasks">E. impossible tasks</option><option value="F. associated risks">F. associated risks</option><option value="G. water level">G. water level</option><option value="H. specific areas">H. specific areas</option><option value="I. total expenditure">I. total expenditure</option><option value="J. construction guidelines">J. construction guidelines</option></select></span></span>. He points out that although the cost of foundations increases when bedrock is deep below the surface, this cannot be regarded as <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">37</strong><select data-qnum="37" name="question_37" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 37 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. development plans">A. development plans</option><option value="B. deep excavations">B. deep excavations</option><option value="C. great distance">C. great distance</option><option value="D. excessive expense">D. excessive expense</option><option value="E. impossible tasks">E. impossible tasks</option><option value="F. associated risks">F. associated risks</option><option value="G. water level">G. water level</option><option value="H. specific areas">H. specific areas</option><option value="I. total expenditure">I. total expenditure</option><option value="J. construction guidelines">J. construction guidelines</option></select></span></span>, especially when compared to <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">38</strong><select data-qnum="38" name="question_38" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 38 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. development plans">A. development plans</option><option value="B. deep excavations">B. deep excavations</option><option value="C. great distance">C. great distance</option><option value="D. excessive expense">D. excessive expense</option><option value="E. impossible tasks">E. impossible tasks</option><option value="F. associated risks">F. associated risks</option><option value="G. water level">G. water level</option><option value="H. specific areas">H. specific areas</option><option value="I. total expenditure">I. total expenditure</option><option value="J. construction guidelines">J. construction guidelines</option></select></span></span>.</p>
<p>A particularly enjoyable part of the chapter was Barr’s account of how foundations are built. He describes not only how <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">39</strong><select data-qnum="39" name="question_39" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 39 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. development plans">A. development plans</option><option value="B. deep excavations">B. deep excavations</option><option value="C. great distance">C. great distance</option><option value="D. excessive expense">D. excessive expense</option><option value="E. impossible tasks">E. impossible tasks</option><option value="F. associated risks">F. associated risks</option><option value="G. water level">G. water level</option><option value="H. specific areas">H. specific areas</option><option value="I. total expenditure">I. total expenditure</option><option value="J. construction guidelines">J. construction guidelines</option></select></span></span> are made possible by the use of caissons, but he also discusses their <span class="ielts-reading-question-item"><span class="ielts-inline-select-wrap"><strong class="ielts-q-badge">40</strong><select data-qnum="40" name="question_40" class="ielts-inline-select"><option value="">[ 40 ] Tanlang...</option><option value="A. development plans">A. development plans</option><option value="B. deep excavations">B. deep excavations</option><option value="C. great distance">C. great distance</option><option value="D. excessive expense">D. excessive expense</option><option value="E. impossible tasks">E. impossible tasks</option><option value="F. associated risks">F. associated risks</option><option value="G. water level">G. water level</option><option value="H. specific areas">H. specific areas</option><option value="I. total expenditure">I. total expenditure</option><option value="J. construction guidelines">J. construction guidelines</option></select></span></span>. The chapter is well researched but relatively easy to understand.</p>
